maluos-estate (0.2.1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * maluos-overlay.service keeps its state in its own directory,
    /var/lib/maluos/overlay (StateDirectory): sharing /var/lib/maluos/estate
    with the daemon let systemd re-own that directory to root when the overlay
    started, after which the daemon (maluos-estate) could not write state.json
    and `maluctl estate status` saw no heartbeat.
  * mock office: a fake spoke (--overlay-spoke) is allocated its address after
    the node and desk tables exist; it crashed the mock at start.

 -- Edward Honour <ed@maludb.org>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 16:20:00 +0000

maluos-estate (0.2.0) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Phase 13 slice 1b, the overlay (docs/phase13-slice1b-overlay.md): the
    estate's own WireGuard network, made when a host joins. `maluctl estate
    join` makes the host's X25519 key (the private half into the vault beside
    the token, estate-overlay-key), sends the public half, records the address,
    the name (NAME.internal) and the hub the office answers with, and starts
    maluos-overlay.service: a root reconciler with CAP_NET_ADMIN that makes the
    `estate` interface, peers it with the hub (keepalive 25 s) or, on the hub
    (`join --hub HOST:PORT`, listening on udp 51820, forwarding on the
    interface only), with every spoke, keeps a marked block of names in
    /etc/hosts, and refreshes from GET endpoints.overlay every refresh_s, at
    once on a heartbeat's overlay_changed (the hint under /run/maluos/overlay)
    or SIGHUP; SIGTERM leaves the tunnel up. `--no-overlay` opts a host out.
    `maluctl estate overlay status|peers|up|down|rotate|refresh`; `leave`
    takes the tunnel down and removes the key. The report carries `overlay`.
    The mock office serves the overlay contract (an allocator, the hub, the
    peers, the names, rotation, overlay_changed, /mock/overlay). Depends on
    wireguard-tools, iproute2, python3-cryptography.

 -- Edward Honour <ed@maludb.org>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 15:00:00 +0000

maluos-estate (0.1.1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * The daemon makes the report on a worker thread; a heartbeat waits for
    it at most half the interval (15 s at most) and a report that finishes
    later goes at once with a heartbeat of its own. The first image chain
    saw the node swept offline between heartbeats while `maluctl update
    status` ran under emulation (heartbeat_s 5, the sweep at 15 s): a slow
    report must never cost the node its presence.

 -- Edward Honour <ed@maludb.org>  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:00:00 +0000

maluos-estate (0.1.0) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Phase 13 slice 1, the host half (docs/phase13-slice1-nodes.md): a
    MaluOS server joins its estate. `sudo maluctl estate join URL --code
    CODE [--name N] [--kind server|inference]`: the host principal
    host:<hostname> (kind service), the system user maluos-estate mapped
    to it in vault.yaml and gateway.yaml, an ed25519 node key and the
    node token in that principal's vault compartment, the enrolment in
    /etc/maluos/estate.yaml (no secret), maluos-estate.service started.
    The daemon heartbeats presence and facts every heartbeat_s and the
    report every report_s (units, agents, the gateway, vault alerts,
    updates, disk, memory, load; under 32 KB; a part that cannot be read
    is null and named in errors); `offline` on SIGTERM; 401/410 handled;
    a retry ladder on network errors. `maluctl estate status|report|
    heartbeat|nodes|leave`; `--kind desk` runs `maluctl office enroll`.
    The mock office (moved here from maluos-desk, same module name
    there) grows the estate contract: join with node codes, heartbeat
    with the report, the health rules and alerts, the presence sweep,
    /api/v1/estate/nodes and /api/v1/desks/estate.

 -- Edward Honour <ed@maludb.org>  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 04:00:00 +0000
